// SPEED ZONE — HAIRPIN — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// SPEED ZONE as eurobeat at 155 — a game alternate of speed, selectable from the dev
// menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// Mountain-pass racing music: four on the floor, an octave bass on the eighths, brass
// on the off-beats, an orchestra hit every fourth bar. THE RIFF (E5 . . B4 . E5 . G5 .
// E5 . B4 . A4 . B4) is the synth riff that opens it, and shifts up through the gears —
// E, A, B minor, as speed.js walks its lap — to build into the chorus. THE SYNTH LEAD
// (speed.js lead4, HORIZON LINES' melody) is the chorus, all sixteen bars of it on a
// bright grand in octaves doubled by a plain square, re-harmonised i III VI VII where the cabinet
// has i III iv v. The solo is the cabinet's own runs (lead10, VAR 3 RUNS) over the
// middle-eight changes the player hears. The last chorus goes up a whole step.
// 
//   1–4    intro — the riff, octave bass, strings, backbeat (the cabinet-screen loop)
//   5–8    riff — the full band
//   9–12   gear shift — the riff in E, A, B minor, a B7 run; roll and riser
//   13–28  CHORUS — grand + square on the synth lead: a, b, a in A minor, a in B minor
//   29–36  solo — the runs over G D Em C Am D C B
//   37–44  piano break — the synth lead on the grand over Cmaj7 D Em7 Am B7; the build
//   45–60  LAST CHORUS — up a whole step, F# minor; the riff rides over the tune
//   61–64  riff — home to E minor; the loop returns to bar 5
// 
// Home key E minor (the coyote howl and the wink are E minor, baked at the song's
// tempo); the chorus lands 12 s after bar 5.
// 
